<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        The quickest way in: open your account's transactions on your bank's website, select them, copy, and paste into
        LoonieBooks. It works out the dates, descriptions and amounts itself, in English or French. Reading happens in your
        browser; only the transactions you import are saved.
    </p>

    <h3>Step by step</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>On <a href="import">Import</a>, choose the <span class="help-ui">Account</span> the transactions belong to.
            (No accounts yet? <a href="accounts">Add one first</a>.) Investment accounts aren't listed: they keep holdings
            instead of transactions; see <a href="help?a=holdings">Investment holdings</a>.</li>
        <li>On your bank's site, show the transactions for the dates you want. Drag across the rows to select them, or click in the
            list and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>, then <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>. Taking in the headings and other text around
            the table is fine.</li>
        <li>Click in the <span class="help-ui">Pasted text</span> box and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>.</li>
        <li>Check what it found (below), untick any rows you don't want, and choose <span class="help-ui">Import</span>.</li>
    </ol>
    <HelpClip Name="import-paste" Caption="Pasting a month of credit card transactions, checking them and importing." />
    <p>
        Rather work from downloaded files or screenshots? See <a href="help?a=statement-files">Statement files and pictures</a>;
        they all end up in the same box and the same preview.
    </p>

    <h3>Checking what it read</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>The line under the box says how the text was read and how many transactions were found. <em>Notes</em> lists lines it
            couldn't make sense of; headings and page footers usually belong there.</li>
        <li><strong>Columns</strong>: when the text is a table, each column gets a picker (Date, Posted date, Description,
            Amount (+/−), Money out, Money in, Balance or Ignore) with its first rows underneath. If one was guessed wrong, change it.</li>
        <li><strong>Numeric dates</strong> like 03/04/2026: if they come out as the wrong month, set
            <span class="help-ui">Numeric dates like 03/04/2026 are</span> to <em>Day/Month/Year</em> or <em>Month/Day/Year</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>In and out swapped</strong>: if deposits appear as spending, swap the <em>Money out</em> and <em>Money in</em>
            column pickers. When the paste includes a running balance, LoonieBooks uses it to tell money in from money out by itself.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The preview</h3>
    <p>
        Every transaction is listed with the category and tax treatment it will get. <span class="badge text-bg-light border fw-normal">remembered</span>
        means it takes the category (and tags) you gave the same merchant before. Untick rows to leave them out; the bar at the
        bottom totals what will go in and out.
    </p>
    <p>
        If the paste included balances, <span class="help-ui">Set account balance to … as of …</span> updates the account's balance to the
        newest one. Then choose <span class="help-ui">Import</span>.
    </p>

    <h3>After importing</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Review their categories</span> opens just those transactions, filtered to the ones needing review.
            See <a href="help?a=categorizing">Categories and review</a>.</li>
        <li>Transactions already in the ledger are skipped, so you can paste overlapping date ranges, such as the last 90 days
            every month, without doubling anything.</li>
        <li>Wrong account or wrong file? <span class="help-ui">Undo this import</span> takes it back.
            See <a href="help?a=undo-duplicates">Undo an import, and duplicates</a>.</li>
        <li>Likely tax claims (pharmacy, dental, daycare, donations, RRSP…) are suggested for you to confirm later on the
            <a href="tax">Tax report</a>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
